<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    Runtime-swappable palette attributes — Colorblind Accessibility Mode
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

    Colour resources referenced as `@color/...` in XML are resolved by the
    asset manager during inflation and CANNOT be swapped at runtime. No theme
    overlay, Resources wrapper, or configuration override can intercept them.
    Only `?attr/` theme attributes are runtime-swappable.

    Colorblind Mode therefore needs every colour it remaps to be reachable
    through a theme attribute. These attributes are the indirection layer:

      • The base themes (Base.Theme.UltimateFileManager / .Tv) define every
        attribute below pointing at TODAY'S existing colour resource. With no
        overlay applied, every converted site resolves to the identical colour
        it renders today — the refactor is appearance-neutral by construction.

      • The nine ThemeOverlay.UFM.Colorblind.<Type>.<Strength> styles remap
        them. Applied via ThemeHelper.applyTheme(), which is the app's de-facto
        base-class hook (74 call sites, one per Activity).

    WHEN THE MODE IS OFF, NOTHING HERE CHANGES THE APP'S APPEARANCE.

    TV is NOT attribute-driven today — it hardwires @color/tv_* ~3253 times
    across 271 layouts. That is why this refactor targets the bounded focus
    seam (the res/color/ ColorStateLists and res/drawable/selector_tv_*
    drawables) rather than attempting a layout-wide sweep.

    NOTE ON format="reference"

    Only the three ufmStatus*Icon attributes are reference-typed. A status
    glyph cannot be expressed as a colour, so it must carry a DRAWABLE; the
    attribute's default points at the existing status drawable so the Off state
    is unchanged.

    WHERE EACH MECHANISM IS EXERCISED — every one of them is used by shipping
    code in this repo, which is a deliberate constraint, not a coincidence:

      • attr → COLOUR is proven: `?attr/` appears ~31 times in a colour
        position across res/drawable and res/color (<solid android:color>,
        android:strokeColor, android:tint).

      • attr → DRAWABLE is proven: 274 uses of
        android:background="?attr/selectableItemBackground[Borderless]".

      • attr → DIMENSION appears NOWHERE — not in this file's consumers, and
        not anywhere else in the project. Before this file existed there was
        not a single `format="dimension"` theme attribute in the codebase.

    THE ONE EXCEPTION, RECORDED RATHER THAN BURIED (added T011)

    The FR-06 marker (ufm_focus_marker_bar.xml) positions its bar with
    `android:width` + `android:gravity` on a layer-list <item>, and is itself
    referenced as a LAYER inside other layer-lists. Measured repo-wide, BOTH
    have zero precedent. They are standard framework features (LayerDrawable
    item width/height/gravity: API 23; minSdk 26) and are expected to work, but
    they are the only mechanisms in this feature that this codebase does not
    already exercise — so they are flagged for confirmation during device
    testing rather than claimed as proven. See ufm_focus_marker_bar.xml for the
    rejected alternative and the reasoning.

    Everything else in this refactor uses only the forms listed above.

    That last point drives two scope decisions taken during T002/T011 research
    and worth not re-litigating:

    1. ufmFocusMarkerWidth does NOT exist. The FR-06 marker is
       ufm_focus_marker_bar.xml: a FIXED-width <shape> whose only variable is
       <solid android:color="?attr/ufmFocusMarkerColor" />. Off is a
       TRANSPARENT colour, not a zero width — so no XML dimension reference is
       needed and "Off" still requires no separate drawable and no branching.
       Marker prominence therefore scales with strength by CONTRAST, not width.

    2. ufmFocusStrokeWidth is resolved PROGRAMMATICALLY ONLY — via
       Theme.resolveAttribute(...).getDimension() in TvFocusHelper, never via
       android:width="?attr/…" in XML. It applies to the ~45 TV cards that
       paint focus in code and have no stroke today, so there is no existing
       value to preserve and no neutrality risk.

    The consequence is that no existing drawable's <stroke android:width> is
    converted. That is intentional: measured across the focus seam the widths
    are 1 / 1.5 / 2 / 3dp, and several files use DIFFERENT widths for different
    layers of the same focused state (selector_tv_card.xml uses 2dp/2dp/3dp as
    a graduated glow). One attribute default cannot preserve those, and
    flattening them would erase the glow structure — and in
    selector_tv_button.xml the only <stroke> is the UNFOCUSED default border,
    which is not a focus cue at all.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         FOCUS — FR-05 (clarity), FR-06 (non-colour cues), FR-07 (unification)
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

         Defaults point at today's TV focus palette. ufmFocusAccent exists to
         unify the two focus hues that currently coexist (yellow for buttons
         and list rows, blue for cards and colour swatches) onto one treatment
         while the mode is on; outside the mode the two-hue behaviour is
         preserved because each attribute keeps its own default.
    -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusFill" format="color" />          <!-- solid fill behind a focused element -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusFillText" format="color" />      <!-- text/icon colour drawn ON that fill -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusGlow" format="color" />          <!-- outer focus bloom -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusStrokeColor" format="color" />   <!-- focus outline colour -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusStrokeWidth" format="dimension" /><!-- outline weight for programmatic cards ONLY; resolved in Kotlin, never via android:width="?attr/…" -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusMarkerColor" format="color" />   <!-- marker colour; TRANSPARENT = marker off (see ufm_focus_marker_bar) -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusAccent" format="color" />         <!-- card/swatch focus accent (the "blue" family) -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusAccentGlowOuter" format="color" /> <!-- blue-family outer focus ring (accent @ 50%) -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusAccentGlow" format="color" />    <!-- blue-family mid focus ring (accent @ 40%) -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusAccentSurface" format="color" /> <!-- blue-family translucent focused fill (accent @ 20%) -->

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         SELECTION — FR-08
         Multi-select rows, active tab, active filter/sort chip, current
         storage volume, current view mode, "currently applied" indicators.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <attr name="ufmSelectionFill" format="color" />      <!-- replaces ufm_selection_highlight -->
    <attr name="ufmSelectionStroke" format="color" />    <!-- selected card/row border -->

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         STATUS & RISK — FR-10
         Success / warning / error plus the storage risk tiers. The paired
         Icon attributes carry a distinguishing GLYPH so the meaning survives
         for a user who cannot perceive the remapped colours at all — this is
         the redundancy that makes FR-10 work for achromatopsia, not decoration.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusSuccess" format="color" />      <!-- from ufm_granted / status_success / policy_green -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusWarning" format="color" />      <!-- from ufm_pending / vpn_warning_amber -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusError" format="color" />        <!-- from ufm_error -->
    <attr name="ufmRiskSafe" format="color" />           <!-- from ufm_risk_safe -->
    <attr name="ufmRiskModerate" format="color" />       <!-- from ufm_risk_moderate -->
    <attr name="ufmRiskManual" format="color" />         <!-- from ufm_risk_manual -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusSuccessIcon" format="reference" />
    <attr name="ufmStatusWarningIcon" format="reference" />
    <attr name="ufmStatusErrorIcon" format="reference" />

    <!-- The four below were added in T014 after MEASURING, not assuming. The
         registry's original comment claimed ufmStatusError covered
         "ufm_error / ufm_denied / tv_error_red" — it does not. Those are three
         DIFFERENT reds, and a layout sweep that pointed them all at one
         attribute would have repainted two of them with the mode OFF:
             ufm_error     #FFC62828  day and night (no night override)
             ufm_denied    #FFC62828  day / #FFEF5350 night  (differs at night)
             tv_error_red  #FF5252    day and night          (a different red entirely)
             tv_error      #FFFF5252  (the same colour as tv_error_red)
         Each therefore gets its own lever, defaulted to the exact resource it
         replaces, so every conversion below is appearance-neutral. -->
    <attr name="ufmDenied" format="color" />             <!-- from ufm_denied (night differs from ufm_error) -->
    <attr name="ufmTvErrorRed" format="color" />         <!-- from tv_error_red / tv_error (both #FF5252) -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusGlassGreen" format="color" />   <!-- from tv_glass_green — translucent status card fill -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusGlassAmber" format="color" />   <!-- from tv_glass_amber — translucent status card fill -->

    <!-- T017: the same trap a THIRD time, and the reason this section is long.

         `ufmStatusSuccess` defaults to @color/ufm_granted and `ufmStatusWarning`
         to @color/ufm_pending — but the app renders FIVE more greens/ambers that
         are NOT those colours. Measured day/night, not assumed:

             resource               day        night      why it cannot share
             ufm_granted           #FF2E7D32  #FF4CAF50  (this IS ufmStatusSuccess)
             policy_green          #FF2E7D32  #FF2E7D32  differs from it AT NIGHT
             ufm_success           #4CAF50    #4CAF50    differs from it BOTH modes
             ufm_pending           #FFE65100  #FFFFA726  (this IS ufmStatusWarning)
             vpn_warning_amber     #FFFFA726  #FFFFA726  differs from it IN DAY
             ufm_progress_warning  #FFEF6C00  #FFFFA726  differs from it in day

         Under a red-green preset every one of these must move, or success and
         warning stay indistinguishable for the user the feature exists for. But
         pointing them at the shared attribute would repaint them with the mode
         OFF. So each gets its own lever, defaulted to the exact resource it
         replaces, and the overlays give them all the same success/warning hue.

         The three *Surface attributes are the translucent pill/alert
         backgrounds that sit BEHIND those foregrounds. They are converted as
         pairs with their foregrounds on purpose: remapping a green label while
         leaving its green-tinted pill untouched trades one colour clash for
         another.

         NOT added, because measurement showed it unnecessary: policy_red is
         exactly #FFC62828, identical to ufm_error, so it converts onto the
         existing ufmStatusError with no new attribute. The blue "info" family
         (tv_glass_blue, policy_accent_light, ufm_accent_light) is deliberately
         out of scope — see the T013 note on tv_accent: blue is the hue a
         red-green-deficient user sees comparatively well, and info is not a
         status or risk signal. -->
    <attr name="ufmPolicyGreen" format="color" />        <!-- from policy_green; differs from ufm_granted at night -->
    <attr name="ufmSuccess" format="color" />            <!-- from ufm_success (#4CAF50); differs both modes -->
    <attr name="ufmVpnWarningAmber" format="color" />    <!-- from vpn_warning_amber; differs from ufm_pending by day -->
    <attr name="ufmProgressWarning" format="color" />    <!-- from ufm_progress_warning; differs from ufm_pending by day -->
    <attr name="ufmPolicyGreenSurface" format="color" /> <!-- from policy_green_bg — mobile success pill fill -->
    <attr name="ufmPolicyRedSurface" format="color" />   <!-- from policy_red_bg — mobile danger pill fill -->
    <attr name="ufmStatusGlassRed" format="color" />     <!-- from tv_glass_red — TV danger alert fill -->

    <!-- Confirm action (checksum verify / compare) — a GREEN button. Green is
         a safety signal that red-green deficiency collapses, so it is in scope.
         Kept separate from ufmStatusSuccess because its resting colour is
         checksum_btn_light_green (#4CAF50 day / #66BB6A night), which differs
         from ufm_granted (#FF2E7D32 / #FF4CAF50) — sharing that attribute
         would repaint the button with the mode OFF. -->
    <attr name="ufmActionConfirm" format="color" />
    <attr name="ufmActionConfirmText" format="color" />

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         STORAGE ANALYZER — FR-11
         Category colours for the usage bar and breakdown. Consumers must
         remain separable from EACH OTHER, not merely from the background, so
         these are remapped as a set rather than individually.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerImages" format="color" />     <!-- from ufm_analyzer_images -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerVideos" format="color" />     <!-- from ufm_analyzer_videos -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerAudio" format="color" />      <!-- from ufm_analyzer_audio -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerDocuments" format="color" />  <!-- from ufm_analyzer_documents -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerApks" format="color" />       <!-- from ufm_analyzer_apks -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerOther" format="color" />      <!-- from ufm_analyzer_other -->
    <!-- The EMPTY TRACK behind the segments. Not a category, but it belongs to
         the same set: a segment boundary is only readable if the surface it sits
         on is separable from it, so the track has to be chosen against all six
         segments at once, not independently of them. The base default is the
         literal this view used to hardcode (#FFE0E0E0), so with the mode off the
         bar is byte-identical to today. That default is genuinely poor in both
         appearances (1.06:1 against the day page, and within 1.26:1 of the
         brightest night segment), which is why the overlays move it. -->
    <attr name="ufmAnalyzerTrack" format="color" />      <!-- from ufm_analyzer_track -->

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         ADDED BY T061 — the colours the FR-21 coverage sweep found that the
         original registry had no lever for.

         These were NOT found by reading the layouts. They were found by
         DERIVING the map (.plans/tools/derive_colour_map.py) and asking which
         hue-bearing colours reach no attribute. A hand-written list cannot
         answer that question, which is exactly how `status_error` and
         `status_success` escaped the first sweep and left 115 files still
         rendering original colours.

         Every one below is defaulted to the EXACT resource it replaces, so
         each conversion is appearance-neutral with the mode off (FR-19).
         Where a colour could have borrowed an existing attribute instead, the
         reason it does not is recorded per attribute — borrowing would have
         merged two distinct signals onto one lever.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- A TRANSLUCENT delete/error wash (alpha 1A day, 33 night) used as
         app:backgroundTint on the four TV delete buttons. It cannot borrow
         ufmStatusError: that attribute is opaque, and an opaque red behind a
         delete icon is a filled red button, not the faint wash the TV design
         uses. The alpha has to survive the remap, so the palette carries a
         translucent resource of its own. -->
    <attr name="ufmDeleteTint" format="color" />

    <!-- `ufm_progress_critical` is value-identical to BOTH ufm_error and
         ufm_denied in day and night. It still gets its own lever rather than
         borrowing one: "critical" and "denied" are different signals that
         happen to share a hex today, and folding them together would make it
         impossible to move one without the other later. Note the asymmetry
         with ufmProgressWarning, which has had its own attribute since T017 —
         giving the warning tier a lever and not the critical tier would be
         incoherent. -->
    <attr name="ufmProgressCritical" format="color" />

    <!-- Shizuku's permission-denied label on TV (activity_shizuku_tv.xml, two
         android:textColor sites). #FFD32F2F matches no existing attribute's
         default in either appearance, so it needs its own lever to stay
         byte-identical with the mode off. -->
    <attr name="ufmShizukuError" format="color" />

    <!-- The blue half of the TV focus family: selector_tv_button_blue.xml
         paints focus #3B82F6 with a #993B82F6 halo. This is NOT tv_accent
         (#0284C7) — a different blue — so it cannot ride ufmFocusAccent
         without repainting the button with the mode off. It belongs in the
         focus family (it is a focus fill and must move with the mode), hence
         the ufmFocus* names rather than a generic blue accent name. -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusBlue" format="color" />
    <attr name="ufmFocusBlueGlow" format="color" />

    <!-- selector_tv_icon_btn.xml focuses its icon button with the focus yellow
         at 70% alpha (#B3FBBF24). This is genuinely different from
         ufmFocusFill (#FFFBBF24) — opaque vs translucent — so the translucent
         case needs its own lever for the mode-off state to be unchanged. -->
    <attr name="ufmFocusFillTranslucent" format="color" />

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         ADDED BY THE T072 REGRESSION — the two note colours, and the most
         dangerous defect this pass produced.

         Both are a DARK AMBER (#FFB45309, "Dark Amber for Light mode") by day,
         and each becomes a DIFFERENT colour at night:

             tv_note_color      day #FFB45309   night @color/tv_button_focused_yellow (#FFFBBF24)
             mobile_note_color  day #FFB45309   night @color/ufm_pending            (#FFFFA726)

         So neither is an alias of its night target. Each merely BORROWS that
         colour at night. Name/alias resolution cannot express that: it followed
         the night alias and mapped them onto ufmFocusFill and ufmStatusWarning,
         whose defaults are the NIGHT colours in BOTH appearances.

         The FR-19 pre-check then passed them, CIRCULARLY. It resolved the
         colour through its alias and compared the result against the very
         attribute the alias resolution had just chosen — the same value on
         both sides. ~95 sites were repainted from #FFB45309 to #FFFBBF24 /
         #FFE65100 with the mode OFF, which is the one criterion this entire
         pass exists to preserve. check_neutrality.py caught it (189 failures)
         where the applier's own guard could not, because it compares against
         the COLOUR rather than against the mapping.

         Each needs its own lever, defaulted to the exact resource it replaces,
         so both appearances are preserved byte for byte. The overlays give them
         the warning tier: every consumer is a note's textColor, tint or
         strokeColor — an informational attention cue, not a focus fill. The
         focus mapping was an artefact of the night alias, not a meaning. -->
    <attr name="ufmTvNoteColor" format="color" />
    <attr name="ufmMobileNoteColor" format="color" />

    <!-- ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         ADDED BY T073 — the state colours that were rendering their hue with
         the mode ON, found by measuring CHROMA rather than by reading names.

         Both were invisible to every existing instrument, and for the same
         reason: the gap report in derive_colour_map.py decides what needs a
         lever from the colour's NAME (green|red|amber|error|danger|...). These
         two names happen to contain no hue word, so neither was reported as a
         gap, and neither was mapped. The file already confesses this blind spot
         for one case ("the note colours were unmapped ... purely because 'note'
         is not a hue word"); these are the same defect with no such luck.

         What found them was check_unlevelled_hues.py, which asks the question
         the name heuristic cannot: does this colour RENDER AS A HUE? The answer
         is a property of the value, not of the identifier.

         `shizuku_status_ok` is the sharper of the two, because the app already
         has the mirror-image attribute: `ufmShizukuError` exists for
         `shizuku_status_error`. The error half had a lever and the OK half did
         not — and the OK half is GREEN, which is the exact hue the user
         reported still being visible with the mode on.
         ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

    <!-- #FF1B5E20, a deep green, set as a TextView colour in ShizukuTvActivity.
         Rides the success tier: "service is enabled and active" is a success
         state, and it is the counterpart of ufmShizukuError, which rides the
         error tier. -->
    <attr name="ufmShizukuOk" format="color" />

    <!-- #FFB71C1C, the red of the stop/abort affordance (4 layout sites and one
         adapter tint). Rides the error tier — stopping a transfer is a
         destructive action, and it is the same role ufmDenied serves for delete.
         It does NOT borrow ufmDenied: that attribute's default is #FFC62828, and
         a different red behind the same action would change the mode-Off
         appearance (FR-19). -->
    <attr name="ufmStopButton" format="color" />

    <!-- #FF00897B, the positive tier of the progress scale, and the tint of the
         success icon (`ic_success.xml`) and the support success dialog.

         It completes a family that had a hole in the middle: `ufmProgressCritical`
         and `ufmProgressWarning` have both had levers since T017/T061, and the OK
         tier had none. That is the third instance of the same asymmetry found in
         this pass — `shizuku_status_error` had a lever and `shizuku_status_ok`
         did not; `policy_green` had one and `policy_red` did not.

         The value is identical to `ufm_primary` (#FF00897B), so this could have
         been folded onto the brand accent instead. It is not, because the two are
         read for different reasons — one is identity, one is "this succeeded" —
         and the accent family is FR-22's layer, which the blind palette must be
         able to MOVE rather than share. -->
    <attr name="ufmProgressFill" format="color" />

</resources>
